{
    "id": "run-locally",
    "question": "Can I run Footnote locally instead of using a cloud model?",
    "response": {
        "action": "message",
        "message": "Yes — the project is explicitly designed to be run on your own hardware. Footnote's own GitHub description says it is \"transparent, private, and easy to run yourself,\" and notes that it uses Docker containers for deployment on your own hardware or in the cloud [1](https://github.com/footnote-ai/footnote). So the application and backend can absolutely run locally rather than as a hosted cloud service.\n\nOne distinction worth making: \"running locally\" can mean two different things, and the docs don't blur them.\n\n1. **Running the Footnote application locally** — supported. Docker-based deployment on your own hardware is the stated model [1](https://github.com/footnote-ai/footnote).\n\n2. **Running the LLM itself locally** — that's a separate question, and I don't have source-level confirmation either way. The architecture docs describe an API client that centralizes HTTP calls, authentication headers, and error handling [5](https://deepwiki.com/footnote-ai/footnote/3.2-api-client), plus observability for model usage and cost tracking [4](https://deepwiki.com/footnote-ai/footnote/2.5-observability). That pattern is consistent with the framework calling a model API, which could be a cloud model by default — but it could equally be pointed at a locally hosted endpoint (e.g., Ollama, vLLM, or a local API server), depending on how the configuration is wired.\n\nSo the honest summary: the Footnote stack itself is designed for local, self-hosted operation via Docker [1](https://github.com/footnote-ai/footnote), while the underlying model backend is configured through an API client layer [5](https://deepwiki.com/footnote-ai/footnote/3.2-api-client). Whether \"local\" includes the model weights depends on your model backend choice — the docs I saw describe the client plumbing, not a bundled local model. For a definitive answer on local-model support, the setup or configuration docs (or a repo source file) would be the place to confirm.",
        "modality": "text",
